999) - The European Commission has adopted a proposal for a Council Regulation on the establishment of a European Foundation for the Improvement of Living and Working Conditions. The Foundation will be based in Dublin and will be responsible for the management of the European Observatory on Safety and Health at Work.
The European Foundation for the Improvement of Living and Working Conditions (Eurofound) was established in 1975 by the Council of the European Union. It is a tripartite body, with representatives of governments, employers and trade unions. Its mission is to contribute to the planning and design of better living and working conditions in Europe.
The Foundation's main activities are:
to provide information on living and working conditions in the European Union;
to carry out comparative research on living and working conditions;
to provide information on industrial relations systems and practices in the European Union;
to provide information on the social dimension of the European Union's internal market;
to provide information on the social dimension of the European Union's external relations.
The Foundation's work is organised around four main areas:
Working conditions and sustainable work;
Industrial relations;
Social dialogue;
Quality of life and public services.
The Foundation's work is carried out by a team of around 100 staff, based in Dublin. The Foundation also has a network of correspondents in all EU Member States, who provide information on national developments in the areas covered by the Foundation's work.
The Foundation's work is funded by the European Union.
